# Make Start/Stop Bot Buttons Functional

Wire the dashboard's Start/Stop buttons to actually manage the `main.py` background daemon as a subprocess.

---

## Architecture

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant UI as Streamlit (app.py)
    participant DM as daemon_manager.py
    participant FS as Disk (data/)
    participant D as main.py Daemon

    Note over UI: Page Load
    UI->>DM: get_daemon_status()
    DM->>FS: Read data/daemon.pid
    DM->>DM: psutil.pid_exists(pid)
    DM-->>UI: {running: True/False, pid}
    UI->>UI: Sync bot_running state

    Note over UI: ▶ Start Bot clicked
    UI->>DM: start_daemon()
    DM->>FS: Clean stale stop/pid files
    DM->>D: subprocess.Popen(python main.py)
    D->>FS: Write data/daemon.pid
    D->>FS: Log to data/daemon.log
    DM-->>UI: {success, pid}

    Note over UI: ⏹ Stop Bot clicked
    UI->>DM: stop_daemon()
    DM->>FS: Write data/daemon.stop
    D->>FS: Detects stop file in loop
    D->>D: Graceful shutdown (MT5, logs)
    D->>FS: Remove pid + stop files
    DM-->>UI: {success}
```

### Why stop-file instead of signals?

On Windows, `SIGTERM` **cannot be caught** by Python — it just kills the process (no graceful shutdown). `SIGINT` only works within the same console. A stop-file checked in the daemon's 10-second sleep loop is the most reliable cross-platform approach for graceful shutdown.

